  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 233 AM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge gets pushed off to the east Saturday as an \nupper-level trough digs into the Pacific Northwest. Strong, \nsouthwesterly flow aloft develops as height gradients increase \nbetween the departing ridge and the incoming trough. The 500mb high \nwill be pushed well off to the east over eastern Texas and Oklahoma, \nbut a large fetch of southwesterly flow is anticipated from the \nPacific Ocean, just off the coast of southern California, all the \nway through the Northern Plains. The 250mb jet streak over western \nWyoming looks to develop around 21Z, placing much of the region \nwithin the right entrance region of the upper-level jet, favoring \ndynamically induced lift. 700mb flow will be quite elevated across \nwestern portions of the CWA Saturday morning before weakening \nthrough the mid-morning and early-afternoon hours. Strong 700mb WAA \nwill be present once more east of the Laramie Range before cooler \ntemperatures arrive ahead of the incoming upper-level trough. \nWesterly flow is anticipated to return by Sunday morning across the \nregion, favoring slightly drier conditions. Another round of showers \nand thunderstorms will be possible Saturday, but the best lift \narrives on Sunday with the upper-level trough. Forecast sounding \nfrom the RAP suggest a stout Inverted-V signature across much of the \nregion, with around 1000 to 1500 J/kg of DCAPE. Therefore, showers \nand storms on Saturday may be quite gusty with increasing potential \nfor microbursts. Temperatures Saturday will be warm again, in the \nlow-80s to upper-90s across the region. Hottest conditions are \nanticipated in western Nebraska, where some locations could be \npushing 100F.\n\nSunday onwards looks to remain quite active across the region as \nupper-level flow become more influenced by the incoming trough. \nSunday may be particularly interesting day, weather wise, as the \nupper-level trough deepens and takes on a more negative tilt as it \ndigs into northern portions of the Intermountain West. Strong 500mb \nvorticity maxima look to eject across the region, though 700mb WAA \nremains very low across the region. Dewpoints remain in the 40s and \n50s, so another active day looks possible Sunday, especially with \nthe enhanced dynamics from the strong upper-level trough. Once the \ntrough moves through, an open wave develops with southwesterly flow \ncontinuing across the CWA. Multiple shortwaves look to push through \nthe flow, so daily precipitation chances are expected to continue \nthrough much of the week. Temperatures look to remain seasonable to \nslightly warm across the CWA.",
